# os/gentoo.capabilities — the Gentoo capability declaration (Core v5, #663/#667).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read (never sourced) by Core's zsh/02-capabilities.zsh into $_CORE_CAP, and by
# maint/dotfiles-maint.sh, which is bash. bootstrap.sh links it to
# $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities via blib_link_os_layer.
#
# Transcribed from core/PORTING-MATRIX.md §"Package-manager commands", Gentoo column.
# Validate with:  core/scripts/check-capabilities.sh os/gentoo.capabilities
#
# FULL ATOMS, NOT BARE NAMES. Every verb below takes `category/name`; a bare `neovim`
# is ambiguous to Portage in a way it is not to apt or dnf. That is why this repo's
# install/packages.txt is a list of atoms and why nothing here abbreviates.
#
# A `#` INSIDE A VALUE IS NOT A COMMENT — the reader keeps it. Notes go above.

# ── package-manager verbs (all required) ──────────────────────────────────────
PKG_REFRESH=sudo emerge --sync
# `-a` ALWAYS ASKS, which is the whole reason PKG_ASSUME_YES is absent below: there
# is no flag that makes this non-interactive, so declaring one would be a lie.
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo emerge -auvDN @world
PKG_INSTALL=sudo emerge
PKG_REMOVE=sudo emerge --depclean
PKG_SEARCH=emerge -s
# equery ships in app-portage/gentoolkit, which install/packages.txt installs
# unconditionally (its own comment there reads `bin:equery`) — so naming it is a
# promise this repo keeps. --packages warns on this line because it compares the
# leading BINARY against PACKAGE names and here they differ; the warning is correct
# to ignore, and it is a warning and not a failure for exactly this case.
PKG_OWNS=equery belongs
# A SCRIPT THIS REPO SHIPS, not a command line — the one archive that needs one.
# Counting on Gentoo means parsing the resolver's answer, and a declaration may not
# reach into Core's internals (Core's own row calls a private zsh function, which is
# exactly what an OS repo must not name). scripts/pkg-pending.sh carries the full
# reasoning, including why `eix -u` is structurally unable to answer this.
# bootstrap.sh symlinks it to ~/.local/bin/gentoo-pkg-pending, which is on $PATH for
# both the interactive shell and the maint runner.
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=gentoo-pkg-pending
# It prints one atom per line, so the default match (`.`) and field (1) are right.

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OpenRC, not systemd: this box has `crontab` and no systemctl. Core's 55-maint.zsh
# has had a live cron arm all along; `cron` became expressible in the schema in #665.
SCHEDULER=cron
# NO SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR: cron's entry lives in the user's crontab, not a file of its
# own. The validator rejects one here rather than letting it read as configured.

# ── how `up` applies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The tree must be current before the resolver runs, and a failed sync ABORTS rather
# than computing an upgrade against a stale tree.
PKG_UPGRADE_PRE=sudo emerge --sync
# NO PKG_ASSUME_YES — see PKG_UPGRADE above; `-a` asks regardless, so `up -y` behaves
# like `up` and emerge does the asking, which is correct.
#
# NO PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL. Portage resolves @world as a graph; upgrading a subset is
# how you strand a consumer against a dependency it pinned. `up -i` REFUSES here,
# which is how this repo says the world set updates as a whole.
#
# NO PKG_CLEANUP. `--depclean` is destructive enough that it must be an operator's
# deliberate act, never the tail of an upgrade.

# ── how `up` counts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A NON-ZERO EXIT MEANS "COULD NOT ANSWER", so Core reports its unknown sentinel
# rather than 0. This is the archive the flag exists for: an `emerge --pretend` that
# cannot resolve (a blocker, a masked dependency, a half-synced tree) is common here,
# and reporting 0 would say "up to date" while Portage is stuck.
PKG_COUNT_EXIT_TRUSTED=1

# ── the scheduled runner ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE. A source-based rolling distro that half-upgrades
# unattended is how you get a box that will not boot — and here the run is hours of
# compilation that nobody is watching, on a box whose resolver may stop to ask about
# a blocker it cannot resolve alone. This replaces the `have pacman || have emerge`
# probe Core used to run. Apply with `up`.

# ── optional tools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A DECLARED LIST REPLACES Core's _CORE_DOCTOR_OPTIN — it does not add to it — so this
# re-states Core's nine and appends the two Gentoo actually differs on.
#
# jj and ast-grep carry ²¹ in PORTING-MATRIX.md's GENTOO cell only (plus Kali's), and
# this repo's bootstrap.sh installs both in an opt-in extras block that `--no-extras`
# skips. That is the exact case #666 could not express from a Core-side list: absence
# here is correct and must not read as a degraded integration, while on Arch, openSUSE
# and Alpine — which package both — it still should.
TOOLS_OPTIN=lnav hyperfine watchexec shellcheck shfmt ouch git-absorb jnv gping jj ast-grep
